Title: Rachel Reeves faces calls to resign over ‘cash-for-HS2’ allegations
Date: 4 February 2025

<i> THE BBC, John Johnson </i>

The Chancellor, Rachel Reeves, is tonight fighting for her political future following allegations that she accepted undisclosed payments from infrastructure firms linked to the HS2 project.

A leaked cache of documents, reportedly obtained by a cross-party committee of MPs, suggests that thousands of pounds were funnelled into private accounts in exchange for "favourable public positioning" on the project's extension to Euston.

The Treasury has dismissed the claims as "categorically false" and "politically motivated," but the revelations have already sent shockwaves through Westminster, with the Prime Minister, Sir Keir Starmer, coming under intense pressure to launch an independent inquiry.

'Strategic advocacy'
The documents, dubbed the "Euston Files" by insiders, appear to outline a series of payments made between June 2025 and early 2026.

At the heart of the scandal is a private consultancy firm that allegedly acted as a middleman between major construction consortiums and high-level Treasury officials. One memo explicitly refers to a "strategic advocacy fee" intended to ensure the Chancellor continued to champion the multi-billion pound rail link during a period of intense budgetary scrutiny last autumn.

The timing of these alleged payments coincides with Ms Reeves' high-profile announcement at the 2024 Autumn Budget to green-light the tunnelling to Euston—a move that at the time surprised many who expected further delays.

Westminster Reacts
The fallout has been swift and brutal:

Opposition: The Shadow Chancellor has called for an immediate suspension of Ms Reeves, stating the public "cannot trust a budget written by someone under such a cloud."

The Government: A Downing Street spokesperson insisted the Chancellor retains the Prime Minister's "full confidence" and that all donations are registered in accordance with parliamentary rules.

The Public: Campaign groups against HS2 have gathered outside Parliament, demanding a "total reset" of the project's oversight.

The BBC has seen several of the ledger entries mentioned in the leak, which list payments totalling over £150,000. However, it remains unclear if these funds were used for personal gain or were intended as political donations that simply bypassed the proper disclosure channels.

Tunnelling on hold?
The scandal comes at a sensitive time for the project. Just this week, tunnel boring machines began their 4.5-mile journey from Old Oak Common to Euston. Industry experts warn that any sudden political shift or a "freeze" on the project during an investigation could cost taxpayers hundreds of millions in delay penalties.

Ms Reeves is expected to make a personal statement to the House of Commons tomorrow morning.
